food security in Denmark,

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:22.050
context of the entire European Union

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:24.125
or for instance in the US.

00:13:24.125 --> 00:13:24.958
And there's two ways of going about it.

00:13:24.958 --> 00:13:29.913
In the US, the American model,
it's all, it's torts laws.

00:13:29.913 --> 00:13:30.810
It's afterwards.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.210
If you're a seed company
and you sell seeds

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:35.160
that turn out not to germinate,

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.410
then you can be sued in court
and the penalties will be

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:39.870
so high that you have
a very strong incentive

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:42.600
to be very careful about
the seeds you disseminate.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:47.600
In Europe, we've somehow I
think a worse system, actually,

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:49.620
I think it's originally
coming from France,

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:50.700
although I'm not sure.

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:52.440
We're gonna do it beforehand.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.680
If you want to hand out seeds,
or if you want as a company,

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:59.220
you want to distribute seeds,
you need to ask permission

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:02.460
for the specific variety, you
need to register your variety.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:04.170
That's the US conditions.

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:06.510
And those are the same conditions
for intellectual property

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:07.710
and for market access.

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:09.600
But here, for market access conditions,

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:12.300
then your variety is registered,
a new variety of weeds

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:15.090
and that per batch of seeds
that you want to sell,

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:17.730
you also need to go through
a government procedure

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:19.950
where they will check
on the batch of seeds.

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:22.440
Now what's happened here
and I think that that may be

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:27.440
another problem of interpretation
in the movie that I see

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:30.300
is at some point you
say Denmark has the most

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:32.430
liberal seed laws.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:34.950
That's I think not true.

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:39.060
That could be the pendulum
coming back because Denmark

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:42.390
and the Netherlands are easily
the two countries in Europe,

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:44.580
I think with the most
intensive agriculture.

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:45.997
- Yeah.

00:14:45.997 --> 00:14:47.882
- The Netherlands is the number one,

00:14:47.882 --> 00:14:51.630
it's the number one exporter,
Denmark has all its dairy.

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:55.740
And they have government and agri industry

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:59.700
is so much involved that
you have a huge degree

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:01.380
of implementation of these laws.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:04.980
If you go to Greece or to
Italy, you have more land races,

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:06.690
agriculturists, more small scale.

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:09.720
It's also like Mediterranean
cuisine, all these things.

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:11.051
- Yeah.

00:15:11.051 --> 00:15:13.403
- And what has happened,
it's 10 years ago in Europe,

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:18.060
NGOs have actually scored
something of a victory

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:19.500
and have been able to put on the agenda

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:22.470
of European parliament
that all those seed laws,

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:24.150
let's say beforehand
when you can cultivate,

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:25.860
are too strict and that there has to be

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:27.690
a conservation corrective.

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:28.523
- Yeah.

00:15:28.523 --> 00:15:29.940
- Eventually the entire
conservation directive

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:32.970
was on the agenda, but it's
NGOs again that torpedoed it

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:35.430
because it wasn't radical
enough, it didn't allow for

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:37.080
enough conservation.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.990
I guess some kind of compromise
but I don't know the details

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:41.670
could have been that a
country like Denmark said,

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:44.040
hey, we've gone a lot
further than many of these

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:46.680
other countries, let's now
try and allow some more

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:48.480
conversation our own
country, for instance,

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:51.270
by not applying European laws as strictly.

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:55.200
And so I think if you say
Denmark has the most liberal laws

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.270
for conservation and for seed exchange,

00:15:57.270 --> 00:16:01.050
I interpret that as Denmark
having gone back from

00:16:01.050 --> 00:16:04.050
precipice of being the most
agro industrial country

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:06.270
together with the Netherlands.

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:08.159
So sorry for the (indistinct).

00:16:08.159 --> 00:16:09.660
- No, it's fine.
- No, but I mean this,

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:14.040
this farmer who was
farming a field for rye

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:17.670
and he was saying, okay,
so we use different seeds

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:19.080
and use different kinds of rye

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:21.930
and it makes different kind of flour,

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:24.210
but I guess he doesn't
distribute the seeds.

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:28.740
So in that sense, you know,
it's within the EU or whatever

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:30.390
rules they are about.

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:33.210
You can't sell the seeds but
there's nothing to prevent you

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:35.700
from actually planting them again.

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:38.141
- [Audience] Sure, small scale
in your own farm and stuff,

00:16:38.141 --> 00:16:38.974
mostly it's that (indistinct).

00:16:38.974 --> 00:16:42.630
- I also say for the
comment on the most liberal

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:45.420
seed legislation, that wasn't
something I personally said.

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:49.443
It was something that somebody
from the Danish seed saving,

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:52.743
yeah, Danish seed savers said as well.

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:57.030
And I think that these
regulations on seed diversity

00:16:57.030 --> 00:16:59.100
do come from national security.

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:01.560
That's something that I've
witnessed in my own research

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:04.290
and looking at this from
more of an environmental,

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:08.430
anthropological lens is that
a lot of the seed legislation

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:11.400
popped up around the same
time that there was a lot

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:15.480
of conversation around eugenics
and that there was this idea

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:17.730
of genetic purity that has emerged.

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:20.640
And if you look at old seed
catalogs, you'll see something

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:25.020
that they will advertise for
with using the same language

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:29.460
that a lot of people within
the Nazi regime used as well.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:33.210
And so I think that while
there is like a biological lens

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:35.130
looking at how we regulate seeds,

00:17:35.130 --> 00:17:37.890
there is also a social
lens that got mixed up

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:40.680
within this as well which
has been destructive

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:45.123
for the genetic diversity
of our food systems, yeah.

00:17:46.487 --> 00:17:49.860
- But so yeah, I don't think
they're so liberal in Denmark

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:52.380
they're just going to, you know, say,

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:54.430
well there's this rule and we don't care.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:00.440
This is a normal Danish
attitude, you know, so.

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:04.080
- [Audience] Yeah, true, true.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:06.390
They're not the most faithful clients

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:07.590
of European participation.

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.810
They say sometimes, say
Brussels, there's other countries

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:12.420
that are more loyal to
Brussels than Denmark, so.

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:13.620
- Right.

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:14.580
That's the same.

00:18:16.423 --> 00:18:21.423
But no, but I mean, if
Monsanto owns the patent

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:26.760
on all these agricultural
seeds, well good for them.

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:29.100
But I mean, they also
own the patent on all

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:34.100
the horrible artificial, you
know, fertilizers, etc., etc.

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:38.940
Roundup I think is made by them also.

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.310
So it seems like, you know,
they have it all sort of

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:42.570
sewn up, you know?

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:46.230
You have to have our seeds and
you have to use our poisons

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:50.370
to make these seeds grow
and it becomes, you know,

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:53.490
maybe a monopoly, which
I do think would be good

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:55.640
if somebody could break
it up a little bit.

00:18:56.910 --> 00:19:01.910
But I don't know how extensive
it is and where it exists

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:07.173
all the time and for everybody.

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:12.000
- Yeah, I feel like in the US context

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:14.460
and probably also for the Danish context,

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:19.260
that most agriculture is, yeah,
using these kinds of seeds.

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:23.460
And I think that the more
radical versions of having seeds

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:27.780
that are heritage varieties
is still a very small scale.

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:31.260
But I think a lot of younger
farmers that I had spoken to

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:34.560
in Denmark, they were
looking at this and saying,

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:36.463
this is not a good system.

00:19:36.463 --> 00:19:39.570
This is not a system that's
gonna be around forever

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:42.900
because it's destroying our
climate and it's not going

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:46.380
to survive through certain disasters

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:49.320
or it's not going to be
very hospitable anywhere.

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:52.400
So they have a bit of an
interest to disturb it as well.

00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:55.410
So I thought that that gave
me a lot of hope that there is

00:19:55.410 --> 00:19:57.360
a lot of young farmers
that are doing things

00:19:57.360 --> 00:19:58.560
a little bit differently.

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:00.420
- Well, I guess also by now
there are a lot of people

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:02.700
who are interested in organic farming,

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:06.202
which there certainly was not before.

00:20:06.202 --> 00:20:08.640
But I don't know, I mean, I
don't know in this country

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:12.480
how big it is, it's getting
bigger all the time I guess.

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:16.353
But it could take many
years before, you know,

00:20:19.208 --> 00:20:22.593
people give up on all these
various chemicals that they use.

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:26.220
So.

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:28.140
- [Audience] Can you talk
a little bit more about

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:32.430
like you mentioned like
the adaptation as well,

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:37.230
that probably needs to happen
even if you like it or not,

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:40.140
with the climate change?

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:44.460
- Yeah, and does adaptation
and climate change broadly or,

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:45.754
yeah.

00:20:45.754 --> 00:20:46.970
- [Audience] No, for the seeds, obviously.

00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:50.910
So I don't know, what path do you see,

00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:55.910
and also I guess as in other issues

00:20:58.110 --> 00:21:03.110
regarding the lower income countries

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:07.200
with respect to the developed ones?

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:08.820
- Yeah.

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:12.270
Yeah, I think that this is like
a really amazing opportunity

00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:17.270
for in situ and ex situ
seeds banks to work together

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:18.780
in developing these seeds.

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:21.690
And this is something that
I witnessed when I went

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:25.500
to the Nordic Genetic
Resource Center in Sweden.

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:30.480
They had this very popular
oat milk company called Oatly,

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:33.420
and they were working with
them and had a whole field

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:35.220
of different oat varieties.

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:39.270
And for researching what
oat varieties are the most

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:43.560
drought resistant or the
most heat tolerant or, yeah,

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:45.450
what produced the most oat seeds.

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:47.250
And so that there is some research there

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:48.240
which I think is helpful.

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:49.943
And these bigger gene banks,

00:21:49.943 --> 00:21:52.890
I think that they have
a lot more resources

00:21:52.890 --> 00:21:57.000
so they have an obligation
to help with companies

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.280
and help with individuals to understand

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:01.293
better adaptation practices.

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:05.610
And I think that this can
be a way also for maybe also

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:09.270
lower income countries to work
with genetic resource centers

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:12.450
to understand how to grow seeds,

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:15.630
but also to rely on the
local networks as well.

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:18.690
Because I think a lot of the
local networks have knowledge

00:22:18.690 --> 00:22:22.020
that these ex situ seed
banks do not because yeah,

00:22:22.020 --> 00:22:24.090
you have a situation
where you have Svalbard

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:27.660
that doesn't have any of
the like cultural knowledge

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:29.491
attached to the seed as well.

00:22:29.491 --> 00:22:31.890
It's not like you have, like
when you scan the barcode

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:35.250
of the seed that's kept up
there, that a recipe pops up

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:39.510
or the history of growth pops
up either, it's just a seed.

00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:44.510
So if yeah, everybody lost
their knowledge one day,

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:47.940
then there'd be nothing
to keep that alive either,

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:50.580
which is why cultural
knowledge is important

00:22:50.580 --> 00:22:52.500
for adaptation as well.

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:54.510
- Yeah, well one of your speakers in here,

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:57.513
I forget which one, the
