00:25:55.710 --> 00:25:58.890
knowledge that's kept up,
then you really wanna be sure

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:01.350
that it doesn't get in
the wrong hands as well

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:04.530
because there's definitely
seed companies that'd be

00:26:04.530 --> 00:26:07.413
interested in having
that kind of knowledge.

00:26:08.421 --> 00:26:10.053
- [Audience] True.
- Yeah.

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:12.480
- [Audience] Yeah, there's also a question

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:13.740
of cultural appropriation.

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:14.850
- Yeah.

00:26:14.850 --> 00:26:17.682
- [Audience] What very
often happens in the,

00:26:17.682 --> 00:26:18.515
so I work actually in Africa,

00:26:18.515 --> 00:26:20.010
I know more about Africa than Europe,

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:22.080
and it's actually seed
companies are not interested

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:23.469
in this knowledge.

00:26:23.469 --> 00:26:25.140
They're not interested
in, most seed companies

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:28.293
are not interested in most of
the crops that are grown by,

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:31.980
traditional crops grown
by smallholder farmers

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:32.813
in the South.

00:26:32.813 --> 00:26:37.440
And what many farmers will do
is they will, for instance,

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:40.050
they will have a food crop which can be

00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:44.040
an international traded crop
also or a crop that you find

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:47.340
in many, many countries like
rice, like wheat or even,

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:48.630
sorry, or whatever.

00:26:48.630 --> 00:26:51.887
And then they will have, for
instance, a cash crop like,

00:26:51.887 --> 00:26:54.360
like (indistinct), but
they will also on the side

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:58.373
have some of their more
traditional veggies or other crops.

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:01.680
So they have a diverse farming exercise.

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:04.770
And seed companies are
not interested in say,

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:08.490
the site's operation
of heirloom varieties.

00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:12.030
This is something that's
really on farmer's time,

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:12.863
on farmer's books.

00:27:12.863 --> 00:27:16.560
Seed companies they're, maximally,
they're trying to replace

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:18.720
this but there's no market
in it, it's too small,

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:19.920
it's too regionally diverse.

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:22.308
They are interested in the
other two, the cash crop

00:27:22.308 --> 00:27:27.308
and the main crops for food.

00:27:27.570 --> 00:27:30.120
And what you see there is
very often that those crops

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:33.150
are not local to the area.

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:37.740
Very often they've come
through colonialism,

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:38.880
they've been introduced in other area.

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:42.000
For instance, potato is a new world crop

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:44.940
but you also find it in many
different places in Africa.

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:47.370
Rice for instance in west
Africa is not indigenous,

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:49.680
or at least not the type
of rice you find now.

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:52.170
But at the same time, what
happens is that the farmers

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:54.390
who are not growing their traditional crop

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:56.272
that they have always been growing,

00:27:56.272 --> 00:27:57.750
what the entire richness
of genetic diversity,

00:27:57.750 --> 00:28:02.520
they are growing all varieties
of the 1950s, 1960s, 1970s,

00:28:02.520 --> 00:28:04.770
that still have been
introduced by the colonizer.

00:28:04.770 --> 00:28:07.110
And the problem is then
that many seed companies

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:09.300
are actually not
interested in these markets

00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:11.670
because they're too small
and farmers are too poor.

00:28:11.670 --> 00:28:14.370
So instead of growing the
newest commercial varieties

00:28:14.370 --> 00:28:17.940
of rice, farmers are growing
very old commercial varieties

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:20.490
of rice that also need fertilizer,

00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:23.553
that are also genetically poor quality.

00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:26.913
But that on top of all
that don't yield enough.

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:31.590
So in many African
countries, in my experience,

00:28:31.590 --> 00:28:34.440
I would say that there's
that seed companies,

00:28:34.440 --> 00:28:37.770
that we need more seed
companies coming in to focus

00:28:37.770 --> 00:28:40.053
on certain of their cultures.

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:46.200
But your story is more around local crops,

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:48.690
the veggies and yeah.

00:28:48.690 --> 00:28:51.843
But for many farmers, their
main cultivation is not in that.

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:53.760
- Yeah.

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:57.600
- No, no, you have to be rich
to do all this experimenting.

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:02.290
I mean, you know, you couldn't
do it on a commercial scale

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:06.510
if you didn't know what was it going to be

00:29:06.510 --> 00:29:07.890
and how was it gonna turn out.

00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:08.790
You could not.

00:29:08.790 --> 00:29:09.623
- Sure.
- Yeah.

00:29:11.130 --> 00:29:14.130
- But then it's like,
maybe it's okay if things

00:29:14.130 --> 00:29:17.370
are on a smaller scale in the future.

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:21.303
Like maybe it's not super
sustainable to have huge,

00:29:22.530 --> 00:29:26.370
huge plots of land that are
just owned by a couple people.

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:30.240
And, yeah, maybe that
is a little optimistic

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:33.510
because I feel like
especially with Bill Gates'

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:37.593
recent decisions to buy
up all the farmland.

00:29:38.460 --> 00:29:43.460
But I think it's gonna have
to be more localized maybe

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:44.663
in the future.

00:29:45.810 --> 00:29:47.396
Yeah.

00:29:47.396 --> 00:29:50.400
- [Audience] But the
thing is that, I mean,

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:53.369
there the question is how to balance

00:29:53.369 --> 00:29:55.230
this competing objective.

00:29:55.230 --> 00:29:59.250
Because in any case you
cannot, and they could not do,

00:29:59.250 --> 00:30:02.223
I mean, some small
farmers, they also need to,

00:30:03.810 --> 00:30:06.150
I mean, sustain themselves.

00:30:06.150 --> 00:30:11.150
So it's not that they might
want to grow different variety

00:30:12.840 --> 00:30:17.840
of plant, but at some point
you also need to be able

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:19.920
to be like (indistinct)-

00:30:20.767 --> 00:30:22.236
- Survive off of that.
- To survive.

00:30:22.236 --> 00:30:24.160
- Absolutely, yeah.

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:27.240
- Well, I mean, this I guess
is the industrialization

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:31.647
of, you know, agriculture,
and yeah, we have that.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:37.650
But you know, there should
be room for experimenting

00:30:37.650 --> 00:30:40.822
and I think it's good your
age is exactly the age

00:30:40.822 --> 00:30:42.320
you should do this.

00:30:42.320 --> 00:30:43.740
- One of the the cool things-
- I'm telling you.

00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:47.497
- I think with the farmer
that he's like standing

00:30:47.497 --> 00:30:49.267
in the middle of the wheat
fields, that I think-

00:30:49.267 --> 00:30:51.390
- Rye.
- Rye, yeah, rye fields,

00:30:51.390 --> 00:30:56.390
is that like he has this
beautiful farm plot that's got,

